/-
# The variety generated by block lattices of finite groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 11.93)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 11.93 (D. M. Smirnov): is the variety of all
lattices generated by the block lattices (Archive, 9.62) of finite groups?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Defs
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs
public import Mathlib.Order.SetNotation

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyKourovka11Problem93Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 11.93 (D. M.
Smirnov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700785 (`KOU-11.93`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The block lattice `C(G)` (Archive, 9.62) consists of the cosets of the normal subgroups of `G` and
the empty set, ordered by inclusion. Blocks are closed under intersections, so the meet is
intersection and the join of `X` and `Y` is the intersection of all blocks containing `X ∪ Y`;
lattice terms are evaluated in `C(G)` that way, without building a `Lattice` instance. By Birkhoff's
theorem a class generates the variety of all lattices iff every lattice identity that holds in the
class holds in every lattice, which is the statement.
-/

/-- **Lattice terms.** Lattice terms in the variables $x_0, x_1, x_2, \dots$ are built
inductively: each variable $x_n$ is a term, and for terms $s$ and $t$ their join $s \vee t$ and
their meet $s \wedge t$ are terms. -/
inductive LatticeTerm
  /-- The variable `xₙ`. -/
  | var : ℕ → LatticeTerm
  /-- The join of two terms. -/
  | sup : LatticeTerm → LatticeTerm → LatticeTerm
  /-- The meet of two terms. -/
  | inf : LatticeTerm → LatticeTerm → LatticeTerm

/-- **Evaluation of lattice terms.** Let $L$ be a lattice and $v$ an assignment of an element
$v(n)$ of $L$ to each variable $x_n$. The value of a lattice term under $v$ is defined by
recursion on the term: the variable $x_n$ has value $v(n)$, and a join $s \vee t$ or meet
$s \wedge t$ has as value the join or meet in $L$ of the values of $s$ and $t$. -/
def LatticeTerm.eval {L : Type*} [Lattice L] (v : ℕ → L) : LatticeTerm → L
  | var n => v n
  | sup a b => a.eval v ⊔ b.eval v
  | inf a b => a.eval v ⊓ b.eval v

/-- **Blocks of a group.** A subset $X$ of a group $G$ is a block if it is empty or a coset $gN$
of a normal subgroup $N$ of $G$ (Kourovka Notebook, Archive 9.62). Ordered by inclusion, the
blocks of $G$ form the block lattice $C(G)$. -/
def IsBlock {G : Type*} [Group G] (X : Set G) : Prop :=
  X = ∅ ∨ ∃ (N : Subgroup G) (g : G), N.Normal ∧ X = {x | g⁻¹ * x ∈ N}

/-- **Join in the block lattice.** For subsets $X$ and $Y$ of a group $G$, their block join is the
intersection of all blocks of $G$ that contain $X \cup Y$. Since blocks are closed under
intersections and $G$ itself is a block, this is the smallest block containing $X \cup Y$; for
blocks $X$ and $Y$ it is their join in the block lattice of $G$. -/
def blockSup {G : Type*} [Group G] (X Y : Set G) : Set G :=
  ⋂₀ {Z | IsBlock Z ∧ X ∪ Y ⊆ Z}

/-- **Evaluation of lattice terms in the block lattice.** Let $G$ be a group and $v$ an assignment
of a subset $v(n)$ of $G$ to each variable $x_n$. The value of a lattice term under $v$ is defined
by recursion on the term: the variable $x_n$ has value $v(n)$, a meet $s \wedge t$ has as value
the intersection of the values of $s$ and $t$, and a join $s \vee t$ has as value the block join of
the values of $s$ and $t$, the intersection of all blocks of $G$ containing their union. When
every $v(n)$ is a block, this is the value of the term in the block lattice of $G$. -/
def LatticeTerm.evalBlock {G : Type*} [Group G] (v : ℕ → Set G) : LatticeTerm → Set G
  | var n => v n
  | sup a b => blockSup (a.evalBlock v) (b.evalBlock v)
  | inf a b => a.evalBlock v ∩ b.evalBlock v

/-- **Kourovka Problem 11.93: do block lattices generate all lattices?** The proposition, asked by
D. M. Smirnov, that the block lattices of finite groups generate the variety of all lattices. Here
the blocks of a group $G$ are the cosets of normal subgroups of $G$ and the empty set; ordered by
inclusion they form the block lattice of $G$, whose meet is intersection and whose join of two
blocks is the intersection of all blocks containing their union. Formally, the proposition states:
for all lattice terms $p$ and $q$, if $p$ and $q$ take the same value in the block lattice of every
finite group $G$ under every assignment of blocks of $G$ to the variables, then $p$ and $q$ take
the same value in every lattice $L$ under every assignment of elements of $L$ to the variables.

By Birkhoff's theorem this is equivalent to the variety generated by these block lattices being
the variety of all lattices. -/
def blockLatticesGenerateAllLattices : Prop :=
  ∀ p q : LatticeTerm,
    (∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] (v : ℕ → Set G), (∀ n, IsBlock (v n)) →
      p.evalBlock v = q.evalBlock v) →
    ∀ (L : Type) [Lattice L] (v : ℕ → L), p.eval v = q.eval v

/--
**Smirnov's negative answer to Kourovka Problem 11.93.** The block lattices of finite groups do
not generate the variety of all lattices: there are lattice terms $p$ and $q$ such that $p$ and $q$
take the same value in the block lattice of every finite group $G$ under every assignment of blocks
of $G$ to the variables, but take different values in some lattice $L$ under some assignment of
elements of $L$ to the variables. Here the blocks of $G$ are the cosets of normal subgroups of $G$
and the empty set, and the block lattice of $G$ is the set of blocks ordered by inclusion.

Smirnov shows that the variety generated by the block lattices of groups does not contain all
finite lattices: for each element $g$ of a group $G$, the filter of blocks containing $g$ is a
modular, even arguesian, lattice.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.),
Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026),
Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 11.93 (D. M. Smirnov); resolution: D. M. Smirnov, Siberian Math.
J. 33, no. 4 (1992), 663-668, Kourovka Archive, Problem 11.93: "No, it is not"; Archive 9.62: the
variety generated by the block lattices of groups does not contain all finite lattices,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted blockLatticesGenerateAllLattices_refuted : ¬ blockLatticesGenerateAllLattices

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BlockLatticeVarietyKourovka11Problem93Wanted
